0fea7352b2885dc7a860860d6319e1046b38a3875" "basis" 1256 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $U={a,b,c,…,z}$ be the universal set and $Y={a,b,c,…,j}$, then $\\overset{―}{Y}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d5abde8eb8680dcbb4dcc70fea7352b2885dc7a860860d6319e1046b38a3875" "basis" 1256 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The complement of $Y,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{Y},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $Y.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the letters $a$ through $z,$ while the set $Y$ consists of the letters $a$ through $j.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $Y$ are the letters $k$ through $z.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{Y}={k,l,m,…,z}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/state.json" "file_sha256" "3d5abde8eb8680dcbb4dcc70fea7352b2885dc7a860860d6319e1046b38a3875" "basis" 1256 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14035973/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7116b907de454021442eeb69caa3508059eadd58f51deffe60cc12e100b5af25" "basis" 1256 "verificatio